{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 28",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 178,
      "text": "MEXICO\n46. The supply side policy agenda should be broadened to unlock Mexico’s growth\npotential. The current administration’s policy agenda has focused on redistribution, trade\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 178,
      "end_char": 366,
      "text": "promotion, infrastructure investment, and trade integration to foster development, especially in\nSouthern states. Flagship reforms include (i) the implementation of the U.S.-Mexico-Canada\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 366,
      "end_char": 560,
      "text": "Agreement (USMCA); (ii) increases in the minimum wage and the subcontracting law to reduce wage\ninequality; (iii) increases in the general social pension to reduce old age poverty; and (iv) the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 560,
      "end_char": 749,
      "text": "development of trade infrastructure in the Isthmus of Tehuantepec (“Isthmus corridor”) in the South\nof Mexico. While this agenda addresses some obstacles to higher productivity and growth,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 749,
      "end_char": 948,
      "text": "additional efforts are needed. Priorities include corruption, crime and rule of law issues; addressing\nhuman capital and infrastructure shortfalls; and lessening labor and product market rigidities.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 948,
      "end_char": 1168,
      "text": "A Budget for Higher, More Equitable Growth\n47. A pivot to a more equitable and pro-growth budget would entail a gradual increase in\nproductive government spending, financed by revenue-increasing tax reforms. To lift the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1168,
      "end_char": 1355,
      "text": "growth potential and reduce socioeconomic gaps, Mexico should address spending shortfalls in\neducation, health, public investment, and social safety nets (IMF Staff Report 2021/2020; IMF\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1355,
      "end_char": 1546,
      "text": "Selected Issues 2019, Paper 2; IMF WP/20/215). Structural indicators such as spending per student,\nthe teacher-to-student ratio, and public health spending per capita are well below the OECD\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1546,
      "end_char": 1735,
      "text": "averages and somewhat below emerging market averages (IMF WP/21/244). The pandemic has\nfurther increased spending needs, particularly in education (following 18 months of school closures).\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1735,
      "end_char": 1923,
      "text": "Separately, there is scope, and a need, to increase revenue through tax reform, to support the\nadditional spending and secure tax buoyancy.12\n48. A gradual, permanent Growth Decomposition\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1923,
      "end_char": 2159,
      "text": "increase in spending of around 2 (Annual averages in percent)\n4\nto 3 percent of GDP could foster\n3\ninclusive growth, reduce\n2\npoverty, and address pandemic\n1\nlegacies. This feasible step would\n0\nhelp make progress in achieving\n-1 Labor\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2159,
      "end_char": 2397,
      "text": "the Social Development Goals Capital\n-2 Total factor productivity\n(SDG; IMF WP/21/244). The gains Total GDP\n-3\nfrom higher spending on\neducation, health, public\ninvestment, and social safety nets\nSources:INEGI and IMF staff calculations.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2397,
      "end_char": 2621,
      "text": "could be enhanced with more\nefficiency in programs. For example, social assistance could reduce the leakage of benefits to high-\nincome groups and eliminate overlaps and coverage gaps with the creation of single beneficiary\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2621,
      "end_char": 2850,
      "text": "12 Based on staff’s macroeconomic forecasts, there is a fiscal gap of 0.2 percent of GDP in 2023, which gradually\nincreases to around 1.3 percent of GDP by 2027. In recent years, the authorities have closed expected gaps through\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2850,
      "end_char": 3055,
      "text": "revenue and spending measures and small changes in the budget deficit target. The revenues generated from staff’s\nproposed tax reforms could also be used to close these gaps.\n24 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 29",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 231,
      "text": "MEXICO\nregistry. Efficiency gains in public investment could be achieved through better coordination across\ngovernment levels, effective national and sector strategies to guide planning, and better multi-year\nbudgeting (IMF 2019).\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 231,
      "end_char": 410,
      "text": "49. While higher oil prices have lowered immediate debt service pressures on Pemex,\nrestructuring efforts need to continue. Changes to the business plan should remain a priority,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 410,
      "end_char": 596,
      "text": "including focusing on production in profitable fields, selling non-core assets, and reforming its\npension scheme. More partnerships with private firms in upstream projects could help in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 596,
      "end_char": 820,
      "text": "maintaining or increasing production capacity at lower cost and reducing risks to Pemex’s financial\nposition and the government budget.\n50. A credible, well-designed medium-term tax reform could generate additional revenues\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 820,
      "end_char": 1006,
      "text": "to finance the permanent increase in spending on health, education and social assistance.\nMexico’s non-oil revenue was nearly 6 percent of GDP below Latin American peers (and only about\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1006,
      "end_char": 1167,
      "text": "half the OECD average) before the pandemic. Actions to strengthen tax administration should\ncontinue (Annex XII) but tax policy reforms would also be essential:\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1167,
      "end_char": 1353,
      "text": "• Value Added Tax (VAT). Eliminating zero-ratings, except for a few essential foodstuffs, and\nrationalizing exemptions and differences in rates increase collection. A comprehensive risk\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1353,
      "end_char": 1560,
      "text": "management strategy and a high-coverage audit process for VAT returns could also reduce the\ncompliance gap.\n• Personal income tax. Eliminating exclusions (e.g., of income on personal business activities and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1560,
      "end_char": 1711,
      "text": "independent services), reducing tax expenditure, and widening the top personal income tax\nbracket. These measures could yield around ¾ percent of GDP.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1711,
      "end_char": 1900,
      "text": "• Subnational taxes. Property taxes could be increased gradually to raise over ¾ percent of GDP.\nUpdating the cadaster and enhancing policy coordination between the federal and subnational\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1900,
      "end_char": 2136,
      "text": "governments would be essential. Simplifying and better enforcing the local vehicle tax could\nincrease revenue for states and municipalities.\n• Carbon tax. While carbon pricing coverage through both the Emission Trading Scheme (ETS) and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2136,
      "end_char": 2325,
      "text": "carbon taxation is high relative to peers, Mexico could further raise the carbon price to levels\nconsistent with the Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs; Black and others, 2021). The\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2325,
      "end_char": 2515,
      "text": "carbon tax remains relatively narrow in scope with a de facto exemption of natural gas.13 Also,\nthe tax rate (USD 3 per ton of CO2) is very low and should be raised to USD 75 by 2030 (along\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2515,
      "end_char": 2698,
      "text": "the lines of the global carbon tax floor proposed for G20 economies by the IMF). Such a carbon\nprice, combined with an expansion of emissions pricing coverage, would raise additional\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2698,
      "end_char": 2918,
      "text": "revenues of close to 1.8 percent of GDP by 2030 (Black and others, 2021). A compensation\n13 The carbon tax is an excise applied to the CO2 emissions of fossil fuel suppliers that exceed the emission rate of\nnatural gas.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2918,
      "end_char": 2948,
      "text": "INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 25"
    }
  ]
}

